WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:05.950
I want you to panic….around the year 2030...we
will be in a position where we set off an

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:11.929
irreversible a chain reaction that will most
likely lead to the end of our civilization

00:00:11.929 --> 00:00:12.929
as we know it…”

00:00:12.929 --> 00:00:17.310
If there’s one consistent message coming
from activists and politicians pushing the

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:22.789
Green New Deal and massive new subsidies for
renewable energy it’s that if we don’t

00:00:22.789 --> 00:00:29.400
take radical action now, life on Earth as
we know it will soon be irreversibly destroyed.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.780
the world is going to end in 12 years if we
don't address climate change.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:34.850
The claim is time is running out.

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:41.260
Science tells us that how we act or fail to
act in the next 12 years will determine the

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:43.489
very live-ability of our planet.

00:00:43.489 --> 00:00:48.180
This fear-mongering is flat-out misleading,
and the findings of the scientists studying

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:53.969
global warming don’t support such alarmist
claims, according to the new book Apocalypse

00:00:53.969 --> 00:00:54.969
Never.

00:00:54.969 --> 00:00:57.930
“Please welcome Michael Shellenberger...I
know you’re an environmentalist because

00:00:57.930 --> 00:00:59.350
you’re not wearing a tie.

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:05.670
okay, but you’re a new kind of environmentalist
because you’re very well coiffed.”

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:10.570
the book argues that deforestation and deaths
from extreme weather are actually declining,

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:15.280
and concerns about environmental damage from
plastics are fundamentally misplaced.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:19.660
Shellenberger, who began his career as an
advocate for more government spending on wind

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:24.550
and solar, was eventually disillusioned after
witnessing the failure of subsidies to fix

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:26.460
the inherent drawbacks of renewables.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:32.450
Named a “Hero of the Environment” by Time
magazine in 2008, Shellenberger is an “expert

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:35.590
reviewer” for the Intergovernmental Panel
on Climate Change.

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:42.200
He also appeared in the 2013 documentary Pandora's
Promise, which was shown at Sundance, and

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:46.509
featured several prominent environmentalists
who have come around to see the virtues of

00:01:46.509 --> 00:01:47.570
nuclear power.

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:53.840
it really took us getting clear about how
big the gap was between fossil fuels and renewables

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:56.340
for us to take a second look at nuclear.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.549
“coal, fracking, and nukes - you’re my
kind of liberal.”

00:01:59.549 --> 00:02:05.479
I interviewed Shellenberg over Zoom about
his new book, and why he believes that environmentalism

00:02:05.479 --> 00:03:56.840
has become a replacement for religion in an
increasingly

00:03:56.840 --> 01:03:06.060
secular world.

